    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you go a bit deeper into the vagus nerve part? like what is actually happening physically during those holds that triggers the relaxation response?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, during the holds in box breathing, there's a sustained pressure on the belly, stimulating the vagus nerve through the abdominal diaphragm. The longer the pressure, the stronger the stimulation of the vagus nerve, promoting a parasympathetic response. This reduces heart rate, lowers blood pressure, and decreases cortisol levels, leading to relaxation and improved stress reduction. It is a neurobiological way of signaling your brain to shift from a stressed state to a more relaxed state. The duration — four seconds for each phase — is important to allow the parasympathetic nervous system ample time to reset your physiology from an alert state to a calm state.",
      "source": "target",
